estations: DCM: biventricular heart failure (dyspnea, edema, cardiomegaly, S3); HCM: dyspnea, chest pain, exertional\r\nsyncope, harsh systolic ejection murmur that intensifies with Valsalva; sudden cardiac death in athletes; RCM: severe right-sided failure,\r\nhigh JVP with Kussmaul's sign.\r\nInvestigations: Echocardiography (ventricular dilation in DCM; asymmetric septal thickness ≥15 mm with systolic anterior motion of\r\nmitral valve in HCM; bi-atrial dilatation in RCM); Cardiac MRI (Late Gadolinium Enhancement for fibrosis/amyloid); Holter ECG.\r\nTreatment: DCM: standard GDMT (ARNI, beta-blockers, MRA, SGLT2i), ICD/CRT; HCM: non-vasodilating beta-blockers (Metoprolol),\r\nVerapamil, Mavacamten, surgical septal myectomy, prophylactic ICD; RCM: cautious diuretics, Tafamidis for amyloidosis.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 5 of 12\r\nQ 14 Cardiac Arrest\r\nDefinition: Abrupt, unexpected cessation of mechanical cardiac pump activity resulting in circulatory collapse, sudden\r\nunresponsiveness, absent central arterial pulse, and apnea or agonal gasping.\r\nImmediate Assessment:\r\n1. Confirm scene safety; 2. Check responsiveness (tap shoulders and shout); 3. Simultaneously assess breathing and carotid pulse\r\n(minimum 5 sec, maximum 10 sec); 4. If pulseless and breathless, activate emergency response (Call Code Blue / EMS) and call for\r\nan AED.\r\nEmergency Management:\r\nHigh-Quality CPR (C-A-B): Rate 100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm, allow full recoil, minimize interruptions, 30:2 compression-ventilation\r\nratio.\r\nDefibrillation for Shockable Rhythms (VF / Pulseless VT): Deliver 1 unsynchronized shock (120–200 J biphasic or 360 J\r\nmonophasic); resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes without pulse check; Epinephrine 1 mg IV q3–5min after 2nd shock;\r\nAmiodarone 300 mg IV (second dose 150 mg) for shock-refractory VF/pVT.\r\nNon-Shockable Rhythms (Asystole / PEA): CPR in 2-min cycles; Epinephrine 1 mg IV immediately and q3–5min; Defibrillation is \r\ncontraindicated.\r\nTreat Reversible Causes (Hs & Ts): Hypovolemia, Hypoxia, Hydrogen ion (acidosis), Hypo/hyperkalemia, Hypothermia; Tension\r\npneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis (coronary/pulmonary).\r\nPost-ROSC Care: Hemodynamic support (MAP ≥65 mmHg), targeted temperature management (32–36°C for 24h), emergency\r\ncoronary angiography.\r\nQ 15 Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR) & Basic Life Support (BLS)\r\nPrinciples: Maintains artificial coronary and cerebral perfusion via rhythmic external chest compressions and ventilations to preserve\r\ntissue viability until ROSC is achieved. Follows the C-A-B sequence (Chest compressions prioritized over Airway and Breathing). Chain\r\nof Survival: Early Recognition & Activation → High-Quality CPR → Rapid Defibrillation → Advanced Resuscitation → Post-Arrest Care →\r\nRecovery.\r\nCore Metrics of High-Quality CPR: Rate: 100–120/min; Depth: 5–6 cm (adult); Full chest recoil between compressions (avoid leaning);\r\nChest compression fraction >60% (target >80%); Avoid hyperventilation (500–600 mL tidal volume visible chest rise over 1 second).\r\nStep-by-Step BLS Procedure:\r\n1. Confirm scene safety; 2. Check responsiveness; 3. Call for help, activate EMS / Code Blue, retrieve AED; 4. Check carotid pulse\r\nand breathing (5–10 sec); 5. If no pulse: begin chest compressions on lower half of sternum, interlocked fingers, elbows locked,\r\nshoulders vertical; 6. Open airway (Head-Tilt–Chin-Lift; Jaw-Thrust if spinal trauma); 7. Deliver 2 rescue breaths after every 30\r\ncompressions (30:2 ratio); 8. Attach AED as soon as available, clear patient during rhythm analysis, deliver shock if indicated, and\r\nimmediately resume compressions; 9. Switch rescuers every 2 minutes (5 cycles) to prevent fatigue.\r\nPART III: CRITICAL CARE, DIAGNOSTICS & EMERGENCY SURGERY (Q16 – Q21)\r\nQ 16 Mechanical Ventilation\r\nDefinition: Supportive intervention using an automated positive-pressure device to assist or replace spontaneous alveolar ventilation\r\nand pulmonary gas exchange.\r\nIndications: Severe hypoxemic respiratory failure (ARDS, pulmonary edema); Hypercapnic respiratory failure (severe COPD,\r\nmyasthenic crisis); Airway protection / coma (GCS ≤8); General anesthesia and shock states to reduce work of breathing.\r\nBasic Principles: Positive pressure ventilation pushes gas into lungs. Parameters: Tidal Volume (Vt, 6–8 mL/kg; 4–6 mL/kg in ARDS\r\nlung-protective strategy), Respiratory Rate (12–16/min), FiO2 (21–100%), PEEP (5–15 cmH2O to prevent alveolar collapse), I:E ratio\r\n(1:2).\r\nTypes & Modes: Non-Invasive (NIV: CPAP, BiPAP via mask); Invasive (via ETT/tracheostomy): Volume-Controlled (VCV), Pressure\r\nControlled (PCV), SIMV, and Pressure Support Ventilation (PSV).\r\nComplications: Ventilator-Associated Pneumonia (VAP >48h post-intubation), Barotrauma/Volutrauma (pneumothorax,\r\npneumomediastinum), Hemodynamic hypotension (reduced venous return from positive intrathoracic pressure), and Tracheal stenosis.\r\nMonitoring: Continuous pulse oximetry, capnography (EtCO2), serial ABGs; Peak airway pressure (<35 cmH2O), Plateau pressure\r\n(Pplat ≤30 cmH2O); Sedation scoring (RASS), ETT depth.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 6 of 12\r\nQ 17 Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) Analysis\r\nDefinition & Significance: Quantitative point-of-care laboratory assessment performed on heparinized arterial blood (radial artery)\r\nmeasuring gas partial pressures and pH to assess oxygenation, alveolar ventilation, and acid-base status.\r\nNormal Ranges: pH: 7.35–7.45; PaCO2: 35–45 mmHg; HCO3-: 22–26 mEq/L; PaO2: 80–100 mmHg; Base Excess: -2 to +2 mEq/L;\r\nSaO2: 95–100%.\r\nInterpretation of Primary Disorders:\r\nRespiratory Acidosis: pH <7.35, PaCO2 >45 mmHg. Due to hypoventilation (COPD, asthma, opioid overdose, neuromuscular\r\nweakness).\r\nRespiratory Alkalosis: pH >7.45, PaCO2 <35 mmHg. Due to hyperventilation (anxiety/panic, pulmonary embolism, early salicylate\r\ntoxicity, sepsis).\r\nMetabolic Acidosis: pH <7.35, HCO3- <22 mEq/L. High Anion Gap (DKA, lactic acidosis, uremia, toxic alcohols); Normal Anion Gap\r\n(severe diarrhea, RTA).\r\nMetabolic Alkalosis: pH >7.45, HCO3- >26 mEq/L. Due to H+ loss (severe vomiting, NG suction, loop diuretics,\r\nhyperaldosteronism).\r\nQ 18 Electrocardiogram (ECG)\r\nBasic Principles: Non-invasive graphic recording of cardiac bioelectrical potentials and vector voltages produced by myocardial\r\ndepolarization and repolarization, measured via 10 skin electrodes arranged in 12 standard leads (6 limb, 6 precordial).\r\nMajor Waves, Complexes & Intervals:\r\nP wave: Atrial depolarization (<0.12s, <2.5 mm).\r\nPR interval: AV nodal conduction delay (0.12–0.20s). Prolonged in 1st-degree AV block; short in WPW.\r\nQRS complex: Ventricular depolarization (<0.12s). Widened in bundle branch blocks and VT.\r\nST segment: Isoelectric plateau phase. Elevation indicates STEMI/pericarditis; depression indicates ischemia/NSTEMI.\r\nT wave: Ventricular repolarization. Peaked in hyperkalemia; inverted/flat in ischemia or hypokalemia.\r\nQT interval: Total ventricular electrical cycle (depolarization + repolarization; QTc ≤0.44s men, ≤0.46s women).\r\nClinical Significance: Diagnostic standard for Acute Coronary Syndromes (STEMI vs. NSTEMI); dysrhythmias (AF, VT, VF, heart\r\nblocks); chamber enlargement (LVH); electrolyte disorders (hyper/hypokalemia); and drug toxicities (digoxin).\r\nQ 19 Shock\r\nDefinition: Life-threatening acute circulatory failure characterized by inadequate tissue and cellular microvascular perfusion, resulting in\r\ncellular hypoxia, anaerobic metabolism (lactic acidosis), and multi-organ failure.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nHypovolemic: Hemorrhagic (trauma, GI bleed); Non-hemorrhagic (burn plasma loss, severe dehydration).\r\nCardiogenic: Myocardial infarction (>40% LV loss), end-stage cardiomyopathy, severe acute mitral regurgitation.\r\nDistributive: Septic shock (bacterial endotoxemia), anaphylactic shock (Type 1 hypersensitivity), neurogenic shock (high spinal cord\r\ninjury).\r\nObstructive: Tension pneumothorax, massive pulmonary embolism, cardiac tamponade.\r\nClinical Features: Systemic hypotension (SBP <90 mmHg or MAP <65 mmHg), tachycardia (>100 bpm; bradycardia in neurogenic),\r\ntachypnea, cold clammy pale skin with delayed capillary refill (>3s; warm flushed in early septic shock), progressive oliguria (<0.5 mL/kg/\r\nh), confusion/coma.\r\nComplications: Acute kidney injury, ARDS, DIC, mesenteric ischemia, MODS, and death.\r\nManagement: Airway & high-flow O2; IV access (two 14–16G cannulas) & fluid resuscitation (30 mL/kg balanced crystalloids for\r\nhypovolemia/sepsis; restrict in cardiogenic shock); Norepinephrine (first-line vasopressor targeting MAP ≥65 mmHg); Dobutamine for\r\ncardiogenic inotropic support; Epinephrine IM (0.3–0.5 mg 1:1,000) for anaphylaxis; Emergency cause-specific relief: blood transfusion\r\nfor trauma, needle decompression for tension pneumothorax, pericardiocentesis for tamponade, PCI for cardiogenic MI.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 7 of 12\r\nQ 20 Burns\r\nClassification:\r\nDepth: Superficial (1st degree: red, dry, painful, heals <6d); Superficial partial-thickness (2nd degree: pink, weeping, thin blisters,\r\nvery painful); Deep partial-thickness (mottled white/red, reduced sensation, scarring); Full-thickness (3rd degree: leathery, charred,\r\ndry, anesthetic); 4th degree (involves muscle/bone).\r\nWallace's Rule of Nines (% TBSA in adults): Head & neck: 9%; Each arm: 9%; Anterior trunk: 18%; Posterior trunk: 18%; Each leg:\r\n18%; Perineum: 1%. (Patient's palm = 1%).\r\nClinical Assessment: Primary survey (ABCDE); Rule out inhalation injury (facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in sputum, stridor);\r\nCalculate % TBSA (excluding 1st degree).\r\nComplications: Hypovolemic burn shock, carbon monoxide toxicity, compartment syndrome from circumferential eschar, burn wound\r\nsepsis (Pseudomonas, S. aureus), Curling's stress ulcer, contractures, Marjolin's carcinoma.\r\nPrinciples of Management:\r\nStop burning, cool with tap water (15–20 min, no ice); Early intubation if inhalation injury suspected.\r\nParkland Formula Resuscitation (>15–20% TBSA): Total Ringer's Lactate in 24 hours = 4 mL × Weight (kg) × % TBSA. Give 50%\r\nin first 8 hours (from burn time), remaining 50% over next 16 hours. Titrate to urine output (0.5–1.0 mL/kg/h).\r\nWound debridement, topical Silver Sulfadiazine cream; IV Morphine; Tetanus prophylaxis; Emergency escharotomy for circumferential\r\nchest/limb burns; Early tangential excision and skin grafting (3–7 days).\r\nQ 21 Septicemia\r\nDefinition: Life-threatening systemic infection characterized by the active multiplication and persistence of pathogenic microbes and\r\ntoxins in the bloodstream, triggering a dysregulated host response and acute end-organ dysfunction.\r\nCauses: Gram-negative bacilli (E. coli, Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, Acinetobacter); Gram-positive cocci (S. aureus/MRSA, S.\r\npneumoniae, Enterococcus); Fungi (Candida). Primary foci: pneumonia, urosepsis, intra-abdominal peritonitis, central line infections.\r\nClinical Manifestations: High swinging fever with rigors or hypothermia (<36°C), resting tachycardia (>90 bpm), tachypnea (>20 bpm),\r\nhypotension (SBP <90 mmHg), warm flushed skin transitioning to cold mottled cyanosis, petechiae/purpura, delirium, and oliguria.\r\nComplications: Septic shock (hypotension requiring vasopressors with serum lactate >2 mmol/L), DIC, ARDS, acute tubular necrosis,\r\nand MODS.\r\nInvestigations: Blood cultures (2 sets drawn prior to antibiotics), paired sputum/urine cultures; CBC (leukocytosis >12,000/μL or\r\nleukopenia <4,000/μL, thrombocytopenia); Serum lactate (>2 mmol/L); Elevated Procalcitonin and CRP; PT/INR, aPTT, D-dimer.\r\nManagement (Surviving Sepsis Hour-1 Bundle):\r\n1. Measure blood lactate; 2. Blood cultures before antibiotics; 3. Administer empiric broad-spectrum IV antimicrobials within 1 hour\r\n(e.g., Piperacillin-tazobactam plus Vancomycin); 4. Rapid 30 mL/kg balanced crystalloids for hypotension or lactate ≥4 mmol/L; 5.\r\nNorepinephrine to maintain MAP ≥65 mmHg; 6. Prompt infectious source control (drain abscesses, remove infected lines).\r\nPART IV: DERMATOLOGY & CLINICAL NUTRITION (Q22 – Q26)\r\nQ 22 Psoriasis\r\nEtiology & Pathogenesis: Chronic immune-mediated dermatosis characterized by accelerated epidermal cell turnover (3–5 days vs.\r\nnormal 28 days) driven by T-helper lymphocytes (Th1/Th17) releasing TNF-α, IL-17, and IL-23. Strongly linked to HLA-Cw6. Triggers:\r\nphysical trauma (Koebner phenomenon), streptococcal pharyngitis, stress, drugs (beta-blockers, lithium, NSAIDs, steroid withdrawal).\r\nClinical Features: Sharply demarcated salmon-pink erythematous plaques covered by silvery-white micaceous scales on extensor\r\nsurfaces (knees, elbows, scalp, sacrum). Signs: Auspitz sign (pinpoint bleeding on removing scales) and Koebner phenomenon. Nail\r\nchanges (pitting, onycholysis, subungual hyperkeratosis, oil-drop sign). Variants: plaque, guttate, inverse, pustular, and erythrodermic.\r\nComplications: Psoriatic arthritis (PsA in 30%), life-threatening erythroderma (>90% TBSA involvement), severe metabolic syndrome,\r\ncardiovascular disease, and depression.\r\nTreatment: Topical: potent corticosteroids (Clobetasol), Calcipotriol (vitamin D3 analogue), keratolytics (salicylic acid), calcineurin\r\ninhibitors (tacrolimus for flexures); Phototherapy: Narrowband UVB (311 nm) or PUVA; Systemic: Methotrexate (7.5–25 mg/week),\r\nCyclosporine, Acitretin; Biologics: anti-TNF (Adalimumab), anti-IL-17 (Secukinumab), anti-IL-23 (Guselkumab).\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 8 of 12\r\nQ 23 Eczema and Dermatitis\r\nDefinition & Causes: Non-contagious inflammatory skin condition histologically marked by epidermal spongiosis (intercellular edema)\r\nand intense pruritus. Driven by genetic skin barrier defects (filaggrin [FLG] mutations), Th2-skewed inflammation, elevated IgE, contact\r\nallergens (nickel, fragrances, rubber), chemical irritants, and S. aureus colonization.\r\nCommon Types: Atopic dermatitis (associated with asthma and allergic rhinitis); Contact dermatitis (Allergic [Type IV hypersensitivity]\r\nvs. Irritant [direct chemical/detergent disruption]); Seborrheic dermatitis (greasy scaly plaques on scalp/face linked to Malassezia);\r\nDyshidrotic eczema / pompholyx (pruritic vesicles on finger sides/palms/soles); Nummular / discoid eczema (coin-shaped plaques).\r\nClinical Features: Acute: intense itching, erythema, edema, clustered papules, weeping serous vesicles, and crusting. Chronic: dry\r\nxerotic skin, lichenification (thickened leathery skin with exaggerated markings from scratching), excoriations, and painful fissures.\r\nManagement: Trigger avoidance; liberal use of ceramide emollients immediately after bathing; Topical corticosteroids (hydrocortisone\r\nfor face; mometasone/betamethasone for body flares); Topical calcineurin inhibitors (Tacrolimus); Oral antihistamines for pruritus;\r\nPhototherapy (NB-UVB); Systemic immunosuppressants (Methotrexate, Cyclosporine) or Biologics (Dupilumab anti-IL-4Rα); Oral\r\nantibiotics for secondary impetiginization.\r\nQ 24 Bacterial, Fungal, and Viral Skin Infections\r\nBacterial Skin Infections:\r\nImpetigo (S. aureus, S. pyogenes): Thin-walled vesicles rupturing into pathognomonic honey-colored golden crusts on face/\r\nperioral zone. Rx: topical Mupirocin (2%) or oral Cephalexin.\r\nCellulitis & Erysipelas: Spreading erythema, warmth, pain, and edema with fever. Erysipelas is superficial with raised sharp borders;\r\ncellulitis is deep. Rx: Flucloxacillin, Cefazolin, or Vancomycin (MRSA).\r\nFolliculitis, Furuncles & Carbuncles (S. aureus): Suppurative hair follicle inflammation and necrotic boils. Rx: warm compresses,\r\nincision & drainage, anti-staphylococcal antibiotics.\r\nFungal Skin Infections:\r\nDermatophytosis (Tinea / Ringworm): Annular erythematous plaques with raised, scaly, advancing borders and central clearing\r\n(Tinea corporis, cruris, pedis, capitis). Rx: topical Terbinafine or Clotrimazole; oral Terbinafine/Itraconazole for extensive disease or\r\ntinea capitis (Griseofulvin).\r\nCandidiasis (C. albicans): Beefy-red intertriginous plaques with peripheral satellite pustules in moist body folds. Rx: keep dry,\r\ntopical Nystatin/Clotrimazole; oral Fluconazole.\r\nViral Skin Infections:\r\nHerpes Simplex (HSV-1/2): Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base (\"dewdrops on a rose petal\") that ulcerate painfully. Rx: oral\r\nAcyclovir (400 mg 5x/day) or Valacyclovir.\r\nHerpes Zoster (VZV reactivation): Unilateral, dermatomal eruption of lancinating pain and grouped vesicles. Rx: oral Valacyclovir\r\n(1,000 mg TID for 7d); Pregabalin for neuralgia.\r\nViral Warts (HPV 1, 2, 4): Hyperkeratotic exophytic papules with black dots (thrombosed capillaries). Rx: Salicylic acid, cryotherapy.\r\nQ 25 Vitamin and Mineral Deficiency Disorders\r\nMajor Vitamin Deficiencies & Manifestations:\r\nVitamin A: Nyctalopia (night blindness), Bitot's spots, xerophthalmia, keratomalacia (corneal ulceration and permanent blindness).\r\nVitamin B1 (Thiamine): Wet Beriberi (high-output heart failure, edema); Dry Beriberi (peripheral neuropathy); Wernicke-Korsakoff\r\nsyndrome (ataxia, ophthalmoplegia, memory loss).\r\nVitamin B3 (Niacin):Pellagra (4 Ds): Photosensitive Dermatitis (Casal's necklace), Diarrhea, Dementia, Death.\r\nVitamin C (Ascorbic acid):Scurvy: defective collagen cross-linking causes bleeding spongy gums, perifollicular petechiae,\r\ncorkscrew hairs, loose teeth, subperiosteal hematomas, delayed wound healing.\r\nVitamin D:Rickets in children (epiphyseal widening, bowed legs, rachitic rosary, delayed fontanelle closure); Osteomalacia in adults\r\n(diffuse bone pain, proximal muscle weakness, pseudofractures).\r\nMajor Mineral Deficiencies & Manifestations:\r\nIron: Microcytic hypochromic anemia, pallor, fatigue, angular cheilitis, atrophic glossitis, koilonychia (spoon nails), pica.\r\nIodine: Endemic goiter, hypothyroidism, Cretinism in infants (severe intellectual disability, dwarfism, deaf-mutism).\r\nZinc: Acrodermatitis enteropathica, impaired wound healing, blunted taste (hypogeusia), alopecia, immune dysfunction.\r\nManagement: Direct therapeutic micronutrient replacement (oral Ferrous sulfate with Vitamin C; Cholecalciferol 60,000 IU/week; IV/oral\r\nThiamine before glucose; Vitamin A capsules 200,000 IU; Ascorbic acid 500–1,000 mg); Food fortification (iodized salt, fortified flours);\r\nDietary diversification.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 9 of 12\r\nQ 26 Protein-Energy Malnutrition (PEM)\r\nDefinition & Causes: Spectrum of nutritional deficiency disorders resulting from inadequate intake/absorption of protein and caloric\r\nenergy to meet bodily growth and metabolic needs. Caused by poverty, food insecurity, faulty early weaning onto dilute starches,\r\nrecurrent gastroenteritis, and measles/TB infections.\r\nTypes & Clinical Features:\r\nKwashiorkor (Protein deficiency with adequate calories): Generalized bilateral pitting edema; \"moon face\"; \"crazy-pavement\" /\r\n\"flaky-paint\" dermatosis; sparse, dry, hypopigmented hair with alternating light/dark bands (flag sign); marked hepatomegaly (fatty\r\nliver infiltration); profound misery, apathy, and anorexia.\r\nMarasmus (Severe deficiency of both calories and protein): Severe somatic muscle wasting and total loss of subcutaneous fat;\r\nwizened, shrunken \"old-man\" facies; prominent ribs (\"accordion cage\"); loose folds of redundant skin over buttocks (\"baggy pants\"); \r\nabsence of edema; voracious appetite.\r\nMarasmic Kwashiorkor: Severe tissue wasting coexisting with peripheral pitting edema.\r\nComplications: Hypoglycemia (<54 mg/dL), hypothermia (<35.5°C), severe electrolyte derangements (hypokalemia, hypomagnesemia,\r\nsodium retention), occult severe sepsis, heart failure, and acute keratomalacia.\r\nManagement (WHO 10-Step Guidelines):\r\nStabilization (Days 1–7): Treat hypoglycemia (10% dextrose), warm child, gentle oral rehydration with ReSoMal (no rapid IV fluids);\r\nsupplement potassium/magnesium; broad-spectrum antibiotics; Vitamin A; initial starter feeding with low-protein F-75 diet (75 kcal/\r\n100 mL); strictly withhold iron during stabilization.\r\nRehabilitation (Weeks 2–6): Transition to energy-dense F-100 diet (100 kcal/100 mL) or RUTF for catch-up growth; add iron\r\nsupplementation; emotional stimulation.\r\nPART V: MATERNAL HEALTH, LABOR & GYNECOLOGY (Q27 – Q32)\r\nQ 27 Physiological Changes in Pregnancy, Antenatal & Postnatal Care\r\nPhysiological Changes During Pregnancy:\r\nCardiovascular: Cardiac output rises 30–50%; heart rate increases 10–15 bpm; SVR drops (progesterone-mediated vasodilation),\r\nlowering diastolic BP in 2nd trimester; supine hypotension syndrome from IVC compression.\r\nHematological: Plasma volume increases 50%, RBC mass rises 20–30% → physiological hemodilution anemia; hypercoagulable\r\nstate (elevated fibrinogen, factors VII, VIII, X → 5x increased VTE risk).\r\nRespiratory & Renal: Progesterone stimulates hyperventilation (mild respiratory alkalosis); GFR increases 50%, lowering serum\r\ncreatinine (<0.6 mg/dL).\r\nAntenatal Care (ANC): Comprehensive healthcare surveillance from conception until labor. Objectives: monitor maternal-fetal health;\r\ndetect and manage high-risk obstetrical conditions early (pre-eclampsia, gestational diabetes, severe anemia, Rh isoimmunization);\r\nprovide prophylaxis (iron-folate, calcium, Td/Tdap immunization); birth preparedness and complication readiness.\r\nPostnatal Care (PNC): Specialized medical care provided during the 6-week puerperium (42 days post-delivery). Importance: early\r\ndetection and management of postpartum hemorrhage (PPH), puerperal sepsis, venous thromboembolism, and postpartum depression;\r\nestablish and support exclusive breastfeeding; neonatal surveillance (jaundice, cord care, immunization); postpartum contraception\r\ncounseling.\r\nQ 28 Stages and Physiological Changes of Labor\r\nThe Four Stages of Labor:\r\n1st Stage (Cervical Dilatation): Onset of regular uterine contractions to full cervical dilatation (10 cm). Divided into Latent phase\r\n(slow dilatation up to 4–5 cm) and Active phase (rapid dilatation ≥1 cm/h).\r\n2nd Stage (Fetal Expulsion): Full dilatation (10 cm) to complete delivery of the fetus. Propelled by Cardinal Movements:\r\nEngagement, Descent, Flexion, Internal rotation, Extension, Restitution, External rotation, and Expulsion.\r\n3rd Stage (Placental): Complete delivery of baby to complete delivery of placenta and membranes (normal ≤30 min). Managed\r\nwith AMTSL: Oxytocin (10 IU IM), controlled cord traction, and fundal massage.\r\n4th Stage (Observation): Immediate 1–2 hours postpartum for monitoring maternal hemodynamics, uterine contraction tone, and\r\nvaginal bleeding.\r\nMajor Physiological Changes During Labor: Uterine muscular action shows fundal dominance with rhythmic contraction (transient\r\nshortening) and retraction (permanent shortening without relaxation), creating a thick upper active segment and thin lower passive\r\nsegment separated by the physiological retraction ring; Cervical effacement (shortening/thinning) and dilatation driven by hydrostatic bag\r\nof waters and fetal head pressure; Hemodynamic \"autotransfusion\" of 300–500 mL blood into maternal circulation during each\r\ncontraction increasing cardiac output and blood pressure; Hyperventilation causing respiratory alkalosis followed by metabolic lactic\r\nacidosis.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 10 of 12\r\nQ 29 Menopause\r\nDefinition & Physiology: Permanent cessation of menses resulting from loss of ovarian follicular activity, clinically diagnosed\r\nretrospectively after 12 consecutive months of amenorrhea (mean age 51y). Ovarian depletion of follicles causes circulating estradiol\r\n(E2) to fall, removing hypothalamic-pituitary negative feedback and producing hypergonadotropism with marked elevation of FSH (>30\r\n40 IU/L) and LH.\r\nClinical Manifestations:\r\nVasomotor: Hot flashes (intense sudden heat, flushing, sweating, chills from hypothalamic thermoregulatory instability), night sweats,\r\nsleep disturbances.\r\nGenitourinary Syndrome of Menopause (GSM): Vaginal epithelial atrophy, dryness, dyspareunia, pruritus, loss of rugae, elevated\r\npH >5.0, urinary frequency/urgency, and recurrent UTIs.\r\nPsychological & Physical: Irritability, mood swings, depression, forgetfulness, loss of libido, breast atrophy, skin collagen loss, and\r\narthralgias.\r\nComplications: Accelerated osteoporosis and fragility fractures (trabecular bone loss from osteoclast disinhibition); Cardiovascular\r\ndisease (atherogenic lipid shifts: elevated LDL, decreased HDL; loss of estrogen vascular protection).\r\nManagement: Lifestyle: weight-bearing exercise, calcium (1,200 mg/day), vitamin D (800–1,000 IU/day), trigger avoidance; Hormone\r\nReplacement Therapy (HRT): Estrogen-only for hysterectomized women; Combined Estrogen-Progestin for women with an intact uterus\r\n(progestin prevents endometrial cancer); Non-hormonal alternatives: SSRIs/SNRIs (Venlafaxine), Gabapentin, Fezolinetant (NK3\r\nreceptor antagonist); Topical vaginal estrogen creams for GSM.\r\nQ 30 Common Gynecological Infections & Uterine/Vaginal Disorders\r\nCommon Gynecological Infections:\r\nBacterial Vaginosis: Dysbiosis with loss of Lactobacillus and overgrowth of anaerobes (G. vaginalis). Thin grayish-white discharge\r\nwith \"fishy\" odor. Diagnosis by Amsel's criteria: pH >4.5, positive Whiff test (KOH), Clue cells on wet mount. Rx: oral/topical\r\nMetronidazole.\r\nVulvovaginal Candidiasis:C. albicans. Intense vulvovaginal pruritus, erythema, thick white curd-like \"cottage cheese\" discharge,\r\nnormal pH (<4.5), pseudohyphae on 10% KOH. Rx: single-dose oral Fluconazole (150 mg) or Clotrimazole pessary.\r\nTrichomoniasis:T. vaginalis (flagellated protozoan, STD). Frothy greenish-yellow malodorous discharge, severe pruritus, \r\n\"strawberry cervix\". Rx: oral Metronidazole; treat partner.\r\nPelvic Inflammatory Disease (PID): Ascending infection (Chlamydia, Gonorrhea). Bilateral pelvic pain, high fever, purulent\r\ndischarge, marked cervical motion tenderness (\"chandelier sign\"). Rx: IM Ceftriaxone plus oral Doxycycline plus oral\r\nMetronidazole for 14 days.\r\nUterine & Vaginal Disorders:\r\nUterine Fibroids (Leiomyomas): Benign smooth muscle tumors. Menorrhagia, dysmenorrhea, pelvic pressure, bulk symptoms. Rx:\r\nTranexamic acid, Levonorgestrel IUD, GnRH agonists, myomectomy, hysterectomy.\r\nEndometriosis & Adenomyosis: Ectopic endometrial tissue outside uterus or in myometrium. Severe dysmenorrhea, chronic pelvic\r\npain, deep dyspareunia, subfertility. Rx: Progestins, OCPs, laparoscopic ablation.\r\nPelvic Organ Prolapse: Vaginal bulge, pelvic heaviness, cystocele/rectocele. Rx: Kegel exercises, supportive pessaries, pelvic floor\r\nrepair.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 11 of 12\r\nQ 31 Hysterectomy and Dilatation & Curettage (D&C)\r\nHysterectomy:\r\nDefinition: Surgical removal of the uterus (Total: body + cervix; Subtotal: body only; Radical: uterus, cervix, parametrium, upper\r\nvagina, lymph nodes). Performed via abdominal, vaginal, or laparoscopic routes.\r\nIndications: Symptomatic uterine fibroids failing medical therapy, refractory abnormal uterine bleeding (AUB), gynecological\r\nmalignancies (endometrial/cervical cancer), severe adenomyosis/endometriosis, advanced uterovaginal prolapse, emergency\r\nintractable postpartum hemorrhage.\r\nComplications: Hemorrhage, ureteric injury (proximity to uterine arteries), bladder/bowel injury, pelvic hematoma/abscess, surgical\r\nsite infection, DVT/PE, vaginal vault prolapse, and psychosexual distress.\r\nDilatation and Curettage (D&C):\r\nDefinition: Minor operative procedure involving progressive mechanical dilatation of the cervical canal followed by scraping and\r\nevacuation of endometrial tissue with a curette.\r\nIndications: Diagnostic: evaluation of postmenopausal bleeding, abnormal uterine bleeding in women >40y, suspected endometrial\r\nhyperplasia/carcinoma; Therapeutic: evacuation of retained products of conception (incomplete/missed miscarriage), molar\r\npregnancy evacuation, temporary control of acute refractory uterine bleeding, polyp removal.\r\nComplications:Uterine perforation (risk of bowel/vessel injury), cervical laceration/incompetence, acute endometritis/salpingitis, \r\nAsherman's syndrome (intrauterine synechiae from over-vigorous curettage causing amenorrhea/infertility), and hemorrhage.\r\nQ 32 Urinary Incontinence in Women\r\nDefinition: Involuntary, objectively demonstrable loss or leakage of urine causing physical hygiene, social, or psychological distress.\r\nTypes & Causes:\r\nStress Incontinence (SUI, ~50%): Leakage on effort, exertion, coughing, sneezing, or laughing without detrusor contraction. Caused\r\nby pelvic floor muscle laxity, urethral hypermobility, and intrinsic sphincteric deficiency (risk factors: multiparity, vaginal deliveries,\r\npostmenopausal estrogen deficiency, obesity, chronic cough).\r\nUrge Incontinence (UUI / Overactive Bladder, ~20%): Involuntary leakage preceded by an intense, sudden, uncontrollable urge to\r\nvoid. Caused by detrusor overactivity (idiopathic, stroke, Parkinson's, MS, UTI, bladder stones).\r\nMixed Incontinence (~30%): Features of both stress and urge incontinence.\r\nOverflow: Involuntary dribbling from a chronically overfilled, distended bladder due to bladder outlet obstruction (pelvic prolapse) or\r\nhypotonic detrusor (diabetic neuropathy).\r\nFunctional: Inability to reach toilet due to severe physical or cognitive disability (dementia, severe arthritis).\r\nClinical Features: Leakage on strain/cough (SUI); urinary urgency, daytime frequency (>8 times), and nocturia (UUI); continuous\r\ndribbling (overflow); perineal skin dermatitis, secondary UTIs, anxiety, and social withdrawal.\r\nManagement:\r\nConservative & Behavioral (First-line): Lifestyle: weight loss, fluid moderation, avoid caffeine/alcohol; Pelvic Floor Muscle\r\nTraining (Kegel exercises): at least 3 sets of 8–12 maximal contractions daily for ≥3 months (first-line for SUI); Bladder training\r\n(timed voiding) for UUI.\r\nPharmacological: For UUI: Antimuscarinics (Oxybutynin, Tolterodine, Solifenacin) or β3-agonist (Mirabegron); Local topical vaginal\r\nestrogen for postmenopausal women; SUI: Duloxetine (SNRI).\r\nInterventional & Surgical: Intravesical OnabotulinumtoxinA injections or sacral neuromodulation for refractory UUI; Mid-Urethral\r\nSling (MUS: TVT / TOT) synthetic mesh placement (gold standard for refractory SUI); Burch colposuspension; Periurethral bulking\r\nagents.\r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\n• \r\nHIGH-YIELD MEDICAL REVISION (05-MARK QUESTIONS) QUESTIONS 01 – 32 MASTER COMPREHENSIVE BANK\r\nMedical & Allied Health Sciences Examination Series • 5-Mark Long-Form Outlines Page 12 of 12\n</USER_REQUEST>\n<ADDITIONAL_METADATA>\nThe current local time is: 2026-09-06T18:55:07+05:30.\n</ADDITIONAL_METADATA>"}
