shed breath sounds and vocal resonance **Investigations** Chest X ray costophrenic angle blunting 175 mL homogenous opacity with lateral upward curving meniscus sign Thoracic Ultrasound Diagnostic thoracocentesis using Lights Criteria for exudates Fluid / Serum Protein 05 Fluid / Serum LDH 06 or Fluid LDH 2 / 3 upper limit of serum LDH ADA 40 U / L indicates TB cytology and microbiology **Management** Treat etiology diuretics for CHF ATT for TB chemotherapy for malignancy Therapeutic thoracocentesis drain 1000 1500 mL / session Intercostal chest drain ICD for empyema pH 720 or hemothorax Chemical pleurodesis talc bleomycin for recurrent malignant effusions --- **Q 04 Pneumothorax** **Definition** Accumulation of free air within the pleural space between the parietal and visceral pleura causing partial or complete lung collapse **Types Causes** **Spontaneous** Primary rupture of subpleural apical blebs in tall young men Secondary COPD bullae cavitary TB cystic fibrosis **Traumatic / Iatrogenic** Penetrating / blunt chest trauma rib fractures subclavian line placement pleural biopsy mechanical barotrauma **Tension Pneumothorax** One way valve mechanism allowing air entry during inspiration but trapping air on expiration generating massive intrapleural pressure **Clinical Features** Sudden unilateral sharp pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea decreased expansion hyperresonant percussion note absent / diminished breath sounds Tension hallmark cyanosis hypotension tachycardia tracheal deviation to the contralateral side and distended neck veins **Complications** Obstructive shock severe hypoxemic respiratory failure hemopneumothorax re expansion pulmonary edema **Emergency Management** **Tension Pneumothorax** Immediate emergency needle decompression 14 16G cannula in 2nd ICS midclavicular line or 4th / 5th ICS anterior axillary line without awaiting X ray **Definitive Tube Thoracostomy** Intercostal chest tube ICD with underwater seal inserted in the safe triangle High flow supplemental oxygen accelerates pleural air resorption VATS pleurodesis for persistent leaks 48h or recurrence --- **Q 05 Lung Abscess** **Definition** Localized parenchymal microbial necrosis and cavity formation containing purulent necrotic debris and fluid **Etiology** Aspiration of oral / gastric secretions containing anaerobes Peptostreptococcus Bacteroides Fusobacterium in patients with impaired consciousness alcoholism stroke epilepsy or poor dentition Necrotizing pneumonia S aureus Klebsiella Bronchial obstruction by carcinoma Septic emboli tricuspid endocarditis **Clinical Manifestations** High swinging fever with rigors and night sweats malaise progressive weight loss cough productive of copious foul smelling putrid purulent sputum hemoptysis pallor rapid finger clubbing dullness to percussion and cavernous / amphoric breath sounds over cavity **Investigations** Chest X ray thick walled solitary cavity with a prominent air fluid level surrounded by consolidation CECT Chest delineates cavity and excludes empyema Sputum microbiology Gram AFB culture Bronchoscopy to exclude obstructing endobronchial neoplasm **Complications** Empyema / pyopneumothorax bronchopleural fistula massive fatal hemoptysis metastatic brain abscess secondary amyloidosis **Treatment** Prolonged antimicrobial therapy 4 8 weeks IV Ampicillin sulbactam Amoxicillin clavulanate or Clindamycin transitioned to oral Postural drainage and chest physiotherapy Percutaneous catheter drainage for refractory cavities 6 cm Surgical lobectomy for massive hemoptysis or suspected underlying malignancy --- **Q 06 Respiratory Failure** **Definition** Inability of the respiratory system to maintain adequate gas exchange defined as arterial PaO2 60 mmHg with or without PaCO2 50 mmHg on ambient air at sea level **Types Causes** **Type 1 Hypoxemic** PaO2 60 mmHg with normal / low PaCO2 Caused by V / Q mismatch and right to left shunting ARDS severe pneumonia acute pulmonary edema massive PE diffuse interstitial fibrosis **Type 2 Hypercapnic** PaCO2 50 mmHg with companion hypoxemia Caused by alveolar hypoventilation / pump failure severe COPD acute severe asthma opioid overdose myasthenia gravis Guillain Barre severe kyphoscoliosis **Clinical Features** Hypoxemia dyspnea tachypnea tachycardia central cyanosis restlessness confusion Hypercapnia bounding pulses warm flushed hands morning headache asterixis flapping tremor somnolence and CO2 narcosis / coma **Investigations** Arterial Blood Gas ABG analysis confirms diagnosis defines acidosis / alkalosis and compensation Continuous pulse oximetry Chest X ray and CT thorax Spirometry **Management** Controlled oxygen therapy target SpO2 94 98 in Type 1 strictly 88 92 using a Venturi mask in Type 2 COPD to preserve hypoxic drive Non Invasive Ventilation BiPAP for hypercapnic COPD exacerbations Invasive mechanical ventilation if severe acidosis pH 725 exhaustion or coma Bronchodilators systemic steroids diuretics and antidotes Naloxone --- **Q 07 Occupational Lung Diseases** **Definition** Preventable non neoplastic and neoplastic disorders caused by repeated inhalation of mineral dusts organic particles fumes or toxic gases in the work environment **Causes Common Types** **Silicosis** Inhalation of crystalline SiO2 in stone cutting sandblasting quarrying foundry work produces fibrotic silicotic nodules and high risk of tuberculosis silicotuberculosis **Asbestosis** Inhalation of asbestos fibers in shipbuilding demolition roofing causes bibasilar interstitial fibrosis calcified pleural plaques and malignant mesothelioma **Coal Workers Pneumoconiosis CWP** Coal dust inhalation leading to anthracosis and progressive massive fibrosis **Organic** Byssinosis cotton dust Monday chest tightness Farmer lung thermophilic actinomycetes in moldy hay **Clinical Features** Insidious exertional dyspnea chronic dry or productive cough melanoptysis in CWP wheezing bibasilar end inspiratory crackles reduced chest expansion digital clubbing and late cor pulmonale **Prevention** Engineering controls wet drilling local exhaust ventilation toxic material substitution Administrative controls worker rotation PPE certified N95 / HEPA particulate respirators Periodic medical screening with spirometry and chest X rays ILO classification **Management** Immediate permanent removal from dust exposure Bronchodilators and supplemental O2 Pneumococcal and influenza vaccination Tuberculosis screening Pulmonary rehabilitation Medico legal compensation --- **PART II CARDIOVASCULAR PATHOLOGY HEMODYNAMICS Q08 Q15** --- **Q 08 Atherosclerosis** **Definition** Chronic inflammatory disease of large / medium muscular and elastic arteries characterized by intimal fibrofatty plaques atheromas narrowing the lumen and weakening the arterial wall **Major Risk Factors** **Modifiable** Dyslipidemia high LDL low HDL systemic hypertension cigarette smoking diabetes mellitus visceral obesity sedentary life **Non modifiable** Advancing age male sex family history of premature CAD **Clinical Manifestations** Coronary stable angina acute myocardial infarction Cerebral TIAs ischemic stroke Peripheral intermittent claudication critical limb ischemia Renal renovascular hypertension **Complications** Acute plaque rupture with occlusive luminal thrombosis aneurysmal dilatation / rupture abdominal aorta atheroembolism and progressive ischemic cardiomyopathy **Investigations** Fasting lipid profile HbA1c hs CRP ECG and treadmill exercise test TMT Carotid Doppler intima media thickness and Ankle Brachial Index ABI 09 Coronary CT angiography and conventional catheter angiography **Management** Lifestyle Mediterranean diet aerobic exercise smoking cessation Pharmacotherapy High intensity statins Atorvastatin 40 80 mg to achieve LDL 55 mg / dL low dose Aspirin 75 100 mg ACE inhibitors tight glycemic / BP control Revascularization PCI with drug eluting stents CABG or endarterectomy --- **Q 09 Thrombosis and Embolism** **Definitions** Thrombosis is the intravascular formation of a solid / semisolid mass of fibrin platelets and cells in intact unruptured vessels during life Embolism is the mechanical occlusion of a vessel by a detached intravascular mass transported away from its origin **Causes Virchows Triad** 1 Endothelial injury atherosclerosis trauma vasculitis 2 Altered blood flow venous stasis from immobility turbulence from aneurysms 3 Hypercoagulability Factor V Leiden malignancy antiphospholipid syndrome oral contraceptives Emboli arise from dislodged DVTs cardiac mural thrombi atrial fibrillation fat fractures air or septic vegetations **Clinical Features** DVT Unilateral calf swelling calf pain erythema tenderness positive Homans sign Pulmonary Embolism acute dyspnea pleuritic chest pain tachypnea tachycardia hemoptysis syncope Acute Arterial Embolism 6 Ps Pain Pallor Pulselessness Paresthesia Paralysis Poikilothermia **Complications** Fatal massive PE with obstructive shock ischemic stroke acute mesenteric ischemia post thrombotic syndrome **Diagnosis** Compression Duplex ultrasound DVT quantitative D dimer assay CT Pulmonary Angiography CTPA gold standard for PE Arterial Doppler and CT angiography **Management** Anticoagulation LMWH Enoxaparin 1 mg / kg SC q12h or unfractionated heparin transitioned to DOACs Apixaban Rivaroxaban or Warfarin target INR 20 30 for 3 6 months Thrombolysis r tPA / Alteplase for massive PE with shock or acute ischemic stroke 45h Catheter thrombectomy surgical embolectomy and IVC filter placement --- **Q 10 Ischemic Heart Disease IHD** **Definition** Cardiac syndromes resulting from an imbalance between myocardial oxygen supply and demand predominantly caused by fixed atherosclerotic narrowing of epicardial coronary arteries **Risk Factors** Dyslipidemia hypertension cigarette smoking diabetes mellitus visceral obesity metabolic syndrome male sex older age family history **Clinical Features** Stable angina retrosternal tightness / pressure radiating to left arm / neck / jaw provoked by exertion relieved by rest or sublingual nitroglycerin within 2 5 min Acute Coronary Syndromes NSTEMI / STEMI severe crushing retrosternal pain lasting 20 min occurring at rest accompanied by diaphoresis nausea dyspnea and syncope **Investigations** 12 lead ECG ST elevation in STEMI ST depression / T inversion in ischemia / NSTEMI Cardiac biomarkers hs cTnI hs cTnT CK MB Echocardiography regional wall motion abnormalities LVEF Coronary angiography gold standard **Complications** Malignant ventricular arrhythmias VT / VF sudden cardiac death cardiogenic shock congestive heart failure papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation ventricular free wall / septal rupture **Treatment** Acute ACS Oxygen if SpO2 90 sublingual Nitroglycerin chewable Aspirin 300 mg plus P2Y12 inhibitor Ticagrelor 180 mg or Clopidogrel 600 mg Enoxaparin Primary PCI 90 min door to balloon or thrombolysis 120 min Long term dual antiplatelet therapy high intensity statin cardioselective beta blocker and ACE inhibitor --- **Q 11 Cardiac Failure Heart Failure** **Definition** Complex clinical syndrome resulting from structural or functional impairment of ventricular filling or blood ejection leaving the heart unable to meet the metabolic demands of tissues at normal venous pressures **Types Causes** **Types** HFrEF systolic LVEF 40 HFpEF diastolic LVEF 50 Left sided vs Right sided Acute vs Chronic **Causes** Coronary artery disease and myocardial infarction most common chronic hypertension valvular heart disease dilated cardiomyopathies cor pulmonale **Clinical Manifestations** Left sided Pulmonary congestion exertional dyspnea orthopnea paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnea PND bibasilar crackles S3 gallop Right sided Systemic venous congestion elevated JVP bilateral pitting pedal edema congestive hepatomegaly ascites **Complications** Acute pulmonary edema ventricular arrhythmias cardiorenal syndrome cardiac cirrhosis mural thrombi with systemic embolism **Management** Lifestyle dietary sodium 2 g / day and fluid restriction weight monitoring Guideline Directed Medical Therapy GDMT 4 Pillars for HFrEF 1 ARNI Sacubitril valsartan or ACEi / ARB 2 Beta blocker Carvedilol Bisoprolol Metoprolol succinate 3 MRA Spironolactone 4 SGLT2 inhibitor Dapagliflozin / Empagliflozin Diuretics Furosemide for volume overload Device therapy ICD and CRT D LVAD or heart transplantation --- **Q 12 Rheumatic Heart Disease RHD** **Definition** Chronic permanent cardiac valvular damage resulting from single or recurrent episodes of acute rheumatic fever ARF initiated by untreated Group A beta hemolytic streptococcal GAS pharyngitis **Etiology Pathogenesis** Streptococcus pyogenes Driven by molecular mimicry streptococcal M protein cross reacts with human cardiac myosin and sarcolemma producing pancarditis with pathognomonic granulomatous Aschoff bodies **Clinical Features** History of ARF Jones criteria Valvular involvement Mitral 90 then Aortic **Mitral Stenosis** Exertional dyspnea hemoptysis malar flush tapping apex beat loud S1 opening snap low pitched rumbling mid diastolic murmur at the apex **Mitral Regurgitation** Holosystolic murmur radiating to axilla Aortic lesions cause early diastolic decrescendo murmur **Complications** Congestive heart failure atrial fibrillation left atrial mural thrombi with stroke and infective endocarditis **Investigations** Doppler Echocardiography shows thickened valve leaflets chordal shortening hockey stick anterior mitral leaflet pressure gradients 12 lead ECG left atrial enlargement / P mitrale AF Chest X ray straightening of left heart border double right contour Elevated ASO titer **Management** Secondary penicillin prophylaxis Benzathine penicillin G 12 million units IM q3 4 weeks for at least 10 years or until age 40 Medical diuretics beta blockers / digoxin for AF Warfarin anticoagulation target INR 20 30 Surgery / Intervention Percutaneous Transvenous Mitral Commissurotomy PTMC or surgical prosthetic valve replacement --- **Q 13 Cardiomyopathies** **Definition** Heterogeneous group of intrinsic myocardial disorders associated with mechanical / electrical cardiac dysfunction not secondary to coronary disease hypertension or valvular disease **Types Causes** **Dilated DCM** Ventricular dilatation with systolic dysfunction LVEF 40 Causes idiopathic titin mutations viral myocarditis chronic alcoholism doxorubicin toxicity peripartum **Hypertrophic HCM** Asymmetric septal hypertrophy with preserved / hyperdynamic systolic function Autosomal dominant mutation in sarcomeric genes beta MHC MYBPC3 **Restrictive RCM** Rigid non compliant ventricular walls causing impaired diastolic filling Infiltrative causes amyloidosis sarcoidosis hemochromatosis **ARVC** Fibrofatty replacement of RV myocardium predisposing to ventricular arrhythmias **Clinical Manifestations** DCM biventricular heart failure dyspnea edema cardiomegaly S3 HCM dyspnea chest pain exertional syncope harsh systolic ejection murmur that intensifies with Valsalva sudden cardiac death in athletes RCM severe right sided failure high JVP with Kussmaul sign **Investigations** Echocardiography ventricular dilation in DCM asymmetric septal thickness 15 mm with systolic anterior motion of mitral valve in HCM bi atrial dilatation in RCM Cardiac MRI Late Gadolinium Enhancement for fibrosis / amyloid Holter ECG **Treatment** DCM standard GDMT ARNI beta blockers MRA SGLT2i ICD / CRT HCM non vasodilating beta blockers Metoprolol Verapamil Mavacamten surgical septal myectomy prophylactic ICD RCM cautious diuretics Tafamidis for amyloidosis --- **Q 14 Cardiac Arrest** **Definition** Abrupt unexpected cessation of mechanical cardiac pump activity resulting in circulatory collapse sudden unresponsiveness absent central arterial pulse and apnea or agonal gasping **Immediate Assessment** 1 Confirm scene safety 2 Check responsiveness tap shoulders and shout 3 Simultaneously assess breathing and carotid pulse minimum 5 sec maximum 10 sec 4 If pulseless and breathless activate emergency response Call Code Blue / EMS and call for an AED **Emergency Management** **High Quality CPR C A B** Rate 100 120 / min depth 5 6 cm allow full recoil minimize interruptions 30 2 compression ventilation ratio **Defibrillation for Shockable Rhythms VF / Pulseless VT** Deliver 1 unsynchronized shock 120 200 J biphasic or 360 J monophasic resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes without pulse check Epinephrine 1 mg IV q3 5min after 2nd shock Amiodarone 300 mg IV second dose 150 mg for shock refractory VF / pVT **Non Shockable Rhythms Asystole / PEA** CPR in 2 min cycles Epinephrine 1 mg IV immediately and q3 5min Defibrillation is contraindicated **Treat Reversible Causes Hs Ts** Hypovolemia Hypoxia Hydrogen ion acidosis Hypo / hyperkalemia Hypothermia Tension pneumothorax Tamponade Toxins Thrombosis coronary / pulmonary **Post ROSC Care** Hemodynamic support MAP 65 mmHg targeted temperature management 32 36 C for 24h emergency coronary angiography --- **Q 15 Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation CPR Basic Life Support BLS** **Principles** Maintains artificial coronary and cerebral perfusion via rhythmic external chest compressions and ventilations to preserve tissue viability until ROSC is achieved Follows the C A B sequence Chest compressions prioritized over Airway and Breathing Chain of Survival Early Recognition Activation High Quality CPR Rapid Defibrillation Advanced Resuscitation Post Arrest Care Recovery **Core Metrics of High Quality CPR** Rate 100 120 / min Depth 5 6 cm adult Full chest recoil between compressions avoid leaning Chest compression fraction 60 target 80 Avoid hyperventilation 500 600 mL tidal volume visible chest rise over 1 second **Step by Step BLS Procedure** 1 Confirm scene safety 2 Check responsiveness 3 Call for help activate EMS / Code Blue retrieve AED 4 Check carotid pulse and breathing 5 10 sec 5 If no pulse begin chest compressions on lower half of sternum interlocked fingers elbows locked shoulders vertical 6 Open airway Head Tilt Chin Lift Jaw Thrust if spinal trauma 7 Deliver 2 rescue breaths after every 30 compressions 30 2 ratio 8 Attach AED as soon as available clear patient during rhythm analysis deliver shock if indicated and immediately resume compressions 9 Switch rescuers every 2 minutes 5 cycles to prevent fatigue --- **PART III CRITICAL CARE DIAGNOSTICS EMERGENCY SURGERY Q16 Q21** --- **Q 16 Mechanical Ventilation** **Definition** Supportive intervention using an automated positive pressure device to assist or replace spontaneous alveolar ventilation and pulmonary gas exchange **Indications** Severe hypoxemic respiratory failure ARDS pulmonary edema Hypercapnic respiratory failure severe COPD myasthenic crisis Airway protection / coma GCS 8 General anesthesia and shock states to reduce work of breathing **Basic Principles** Positive pressure ventilation pushes gas into lungs Parameters Tidal Volume Vt 6 8 mL / kg 4 6 mL / kg in ARDS lung protective strategy Respiratory Rate 12 16 / min FiO2 21 100 PEEP 5 15 cmH2O to prevent alveolar collapse I E ratio 1 2 **Types Modes** Non Invasive NIV CPAP BiPAP via mask Invasive via ETT / tracheostomy Volume Controlled VCV Pressure Controlled PCV SIMV and Pressure Support Ventilation PSV **Complications** Ventilator Associated Pneumonia VAP 48h post intubation Barotrauma / Volutrauma pneumothorax pneumomediastinum Hemodynamic hypotension reduced venous return from positive intrathoracic pressure and Tracheal stenosis **Monitoring** Continuous pulse oximetry capnography EtCO2 serial ABGs Peak airway pressure 35 cmH2O Plateau pressure Pplat 30 cmH2O Sedation scoring RASS ETT depth --- **Q 17 Arterial Blood Gas ABG Analysis** **Definition Significance** Quantitative point of care laboratory assessment performed on heparinized arterial blood radial artery measuring gas partial pressures and pH to assess oxygenation alveolar ventilation and acid base status **Normal Ranges** pH 735 745 PaCO2 35 45 mmHg HCO3 22 26 mEq / L PaO2 80 100 mmHg Base Excess 2 to 2 mEq / L SaO2 95 100 **Interpretation of Primary Disorders** **Respiratory Acidosis** pH 735 PaCO2 45 mmHg Due to hypoventilation COPD asthma opioid overdose neuromuscular weakness **Respiratory Alkalosis** pH 745 PaCO2 35 mmHg Due to hyperventilation anxiety / panic pulmonary embolism early salicylate toxicity sepsis **Metabolic Acidosis** pH 735 HCO3 22 mEq / L High Anion Gap DKA lactic acidosis uremia toxic alcohols Normal Anion Gap severe diarrhea RTA **Metabolic Alkalosis** pH 745 HCO3 26 mEq / L Due to H loss severe vomiting NG suction loop diuretics hyperaldosteronism --- **Q 18 Electrocardiogram ECG** **Basic Principles** Non invasive graphic recording of cardiac bioelectrical potentials and vector voltages produced by myocardial depolarization and repolarization measured via 10 skin electrodes arranged in 12 standard leads 6 limb 6 precordial **Major Waves Complexes Intervals** **P wave** Atrial depolarization 012s 25 mm **PR interval** AV nodal conduction delay 012 020s Prolonged in 1st degree AV block short in WPW **QRS complex** Ventricular depolarization 012s Widened in bundle branch blocks and VT **ST segment** Isoelectric plateau phase Elevation indicates STEMI / pericarditis depression indicates ischemia / NSTEMI **T wave** Ventricular repolarization Peaked in hyperkalemia inverted / flat in ischemia or hypokalemia **QT interval** Total ventricular electrical cycle depolarization repolarization QTc 044s men 046s women **Clinical Significance** Diagnostic standard for Acute Coronary Syndromes STEMI vs NSTEMI dysrhythmias AF VT VF heart blocks chamber enlargement LVH electrolyte disorders hyper / hypokalemia and drug toxicities digoxin --- **Q 19 Shock** **Definition** Life threatening acute circulatory failure characterized by inadequate tissue and cellular microvascular perfusion resulting in cellular hypoxia anaerobic metabolism lactic acidosis and multi organ failure **Types Causes** **Hypovolemic** Hemorrhagic trauma GI bleed Non hemorrhagic burn plasma loss severe dehydration **Cardiogenic** Myocardial infarction 40 LV loss end stage cardiomyopathy severe acute mitral regurgitation **Distributive** Septic shock bacterial endotoxemia anaphylactic shock Type 1 hypersensitivity neurogenic shock high spinal cord injury **Obstructive** Tension pneumothorax massive pulmonary embolism cardiac tamponade **Clinical Features** Systemic hypotension SBP 90 mmHg or MAP 65 mmHg tachycardia 100 bpm bradycardia in neurogenic tachypnea cold clammy pale skin with delayed capillary refill 3s warm flushed in early septic shock progressive oliguria 05 mL / kg / h confusion / coma **Complications** Acute kidney injury ARDS DIC mesenteric ischemia MODS and death **Management** Airway high flow O2 IV access two 14 16G cannulas fluid resuscitation 30 mL / kg balanced crystalloids for hypovolemia / sepsis restrict in cardiogenic shock Norepinephrine first line vasopressor targeting MAP 65 mmHg Dobutamine for cardiogenic inotropic support Epinephrine IM 03 05 mg 1 1000 for anaphylaxis Emergency cause specific relief blood transfusion for trauma needle decompression for tension pneumothorax pericardiocentesis for tamponade PCI for cardiogenic MI --- **Q 20 Burns** **Classification** **Depth** Superficial 1st degree red dry painful heals 6d Superficial partial thickness 2nd degree pink weeping thin blisters very painful Deep partial thickness mottled white / red reduced sensation scarring Full thickness 3rd degree leathery charred dry anesthetic 4th degree involves muscle / bone **Wallaces Rule of Nines TBSA in adults** Head neck 9 Each arm 9 Anterior trunk 18 Posterior trunk 18 Each leg 18 Perineum 1 Patients palm 1 **Clinical Assessment** Primary survey ABCDE Rule out inhalation injury facial burns singed nasal hairs soot in sputum stridor Calculate TBSA excluding 1st degree **Complications** Hypovolemic burn shock carbon monoxide toxicity compartment syndrome from circumferential eschar burn wound sepsis Pseudomonas S aureus Curlings stress ulcer contractures Marjolins carcinoma **Principles of Management** Stop burning cool with tap water 15 20 min no ice Early intubation if inhalation injury suspected **Parkland Formula Resuscitation 15 20 TBSA** Total Ringers Lactate in 24 hours 4 mL Weight kg TBSA Give 50 in first 8 hours from burn time remaining 50 over next 16 hours Titrate to urine output 05 10 mL / kg / h Wound debridement topical Silver Sulfadiazine cream IV Morphine Tetanus prophylaxis Emergency escharotomy for circumferential chest / limb burns Early tangential excision and skin grafting 3 7 days --- **Q 21 Septicemia** **Definition** Life threatening systemic infection characterized by the active multiplication and persistence of pathogenic microbes and toxins in the bloodstream triggering a dysregulated host response and acute end organ dysfunction **Causes** Gram negative bacilli E coli Klebsiella Pseudomonas Acinetobacter Gram positive cocci S aureus / MRSA S pneumoniae Enterococcus Fungi Candida Primary foci pneumonia urosepsis intra abdominal peritonitis central line infections **Clinical Manifestations** High swinging fever with rigors or hypothermia 36 C resting tachycardia 90 bpm tachypnea 20 bpm hypotension SBP 90 mmHg warm flushed skin transitioning to cold mottled cyanosis petechiae / purpura delirium and oliguria **Complications** Septic shock hypotension requiring vasopressors with serum lactate 2 mmol / L DIC ARDS acute tubular necrosis and MODS **Investigations** Blood cultures 2 sets drawn prior to antibiotics paired sputum / urine cultures CBC leukocytosis 12000 / uL or leukopenia 4000 / uL thrombocytopenia Serum lactate 2 mmol / L Elevated Procalcitonin and CRP PT / INR aPTT D dimer **Management Surviving Sepsis Hour 1 Bundle** 1 Measure blood lactate 2 Blood cultures before antibiotics 3 Administer empiric broad spectrum IV antimicrobials within 1 hour eg Piperacillin tazobactam plus Vancomycin 4 Rapid 30 mL / kg balanced crystalloids for hypotension or lactate 4 mmol / L 5 Norepinephrine to maintain MAP 65 mmHg 6 Prompt infectious source control drain abscesses remove infected lines --- **PART IV DERMATOLOGY CLINICAL NUTRITION Q22 Q26** --- **Q 22 Psoriasis** **Etiology Pathogenesis** Chronic immune mediated dermatosis characterized by accelerated epidermal cell turnover 3 5 days vs normal 28 days driven by T helper lymphocytes Th1 / Th17 releasing TNF alpha IL 17 and IL 23 Strongly linked to HLA Cw6 Triggers physical trauma Koebner phenomenon streptococcal pharyngitis stress drugs beta blockers lithium NSAIDs steroid withdrawal **Clinical Features** Sharply demarcated salmon pink erythematous plaques covered by silvery white micaceous scales on extensor surfaces knees elbows scalp sacrum Signs Auspitz sign pinpoint bleeding on removing scales and Koebner phenomenon Nail changes pitting onycholysis subungual hyperkeratosis oil drop sign Variants plaque guttate inverse pustular and erythrodermic **Complications** Psoriatic arthritis PsA in 30 life threatening erythroderma 90 TBSA involvement severe metabolic syndrome cardiovascular disease and depression **Treatment** Topical potent corticosteroids Clobetasol Calcipotriol vitamin D3 analogue keratolytics salicylic acid calcineurin inhibitors tacrolimus for flexures Phototherapy Narrowband UVB 311 nm or PUVA Systemic Methotrexate 75 25 mg / week Cyclosporine Acitretin Biologics anti TNF Adalimumab anti IL 17 Secukinumab anti IL 23 Guselkumab --- **Q 23 Eczema and Dermatitis** **Definition Causes** Non contagious inflammatory skin condition histologically marked by epidermal spongiosis intercellular edema and intense pruritus Driven by genetic skin barrier defects filaggrin FLG mutations Th2 skewed inflammation elevated IgE contact allergens nickel fragrances rubber chemical irritants and S aureus colonization **Common Types** Atopic dermatitis associated with asthma and allergic rhinitis Contact dermatitis Allergic Type IV hypersensitivity vs Irritant direct chemical / detergent disruption Seborrheic dermatitis greasy scaly plaques on scalp / face linked to Malassezia Dyshidrotic eczema / pompholyx pruritic vesicles on finger sides / palms / soles Nummular / discoid eczema coin shaped plaques **Clinical Features** Acute intense itching erythema edema clustered papules weeping serous vesicles and crusting Chronic dry xerotic skin lichenification thickened leathery skin with exaggerated markings from scratching excoriations and painful fissures **Management** Trigger avoidance liberal use of ceramide emollients immediately after bathing Topical corticosteroids hydrocortisone for face mometasone / betamethasone for body flares Topical calcineurin inhibitors Tacrolimus Oral antihistamines for pruritus Phototherapy NB UVB Systemic immunosuppressants Methotrexate Cyclosporine or Biologics Dupilumab anti IL 4Ra Oral antibiotics for secondary impetiginization --- **Q 24 Bacterial Fungal and Viral Skin Infections** **Bacterial Skin Infections** **Impetigo S aureus S pyogenes** Thin walled vesicles rupturing into pathognomonic honey colored golden crusts on face / perioral zone Rx topical Mupirocin 2 or oral Cephalexin **Cellulitis Erysipelas** Spreading erythema warmth pain and edema with fever Erysipelas is superficial with raised sharp borders cellulitis is deep Rx Flucloxacillin Cefazolin or Vancomycin MRSA **Folliculitis Furuncles Carbuncles S aureus** Suppurative hair follicle inflammation and necrotic boils Rx warm compresses incision drainage anti staphylococcal antibiotics **Fungal Skin Infections** **Dermatophytosis Tinea / Ringworm** Annular erythematous plaques with raised scaly advancing borders and central clearing Tinea corporis cruris pedis capitis Rx topical Terbinafine or Clotrimazole oral Terbinafine / Itraconazole for extensive disease or tinea capitis Griseofulvin **Candidiasis C albicans** Beefy red intertriginous plaques with peripheral satellite pustules in moist body folds Rx keep dry topical Nystatin / Clotrimazole oral Fluconazole **Viral Skin Infections** **Herpes Simplex HSV 1 / 2** Grouped vesicles on an erythematous base dewdrops on a rose petal that ulcerate painfully Rx oral Acyclovir 400 mg 5x / day or Valacyclovir **Herpes Zoster VZV reactivation** Unilateral dermatomal eruption of lancinating pain and grouped vesicles Rx oral Valacyclovir 1000 mg TID for 7d Pregabalin for neuralgia **Viral Warts HPV 1 2 4** Hyperkeratotic exophytic papules with black dots thrombosed capillaries Rx Salicylic acid cryotherapy --- **Q 25 Vitamin and Mineral Deficiency Disorders** **Major Vitamin Deficiencies Manifestations** **Vitamin A** Nyctalopia night blindness Bitots spots xerophthalmia keratomalacia corneal ulceration and permanent blindness **Vitamin B1 Thiamine** Wet Beriberi high output heart failure edema Dry Beriberi peripheral neuropathy Wernicke Korsakoff syndrome ataxia ophthalmoplegia memory loss **Vitamin B3 Niacin** Pellagra 4 Ds Photosensitive Dermatitis Casals necklace Diarrhea Dementia Death **Vitamin C Ascorbic acid** Scurvy defective collagen cross linking causes bleeding spongy gums perifollicular petechiae corkscrew hairs loose teeth subperiosteal hematomas delayed wound healing **Vitamin D** Rickets in children epiphyseal widening bowed legs rachitic rosary delayed fontanelle closure Osteomalacia in adults diffuse bone pain proximal muscle weakness pseudofractures **Major Mineral Deficiencies Manifestations** **Iron** Microcytic hypochromic anemia pallor fatigue angular cheilitis atrophic glossitis koilonychia spoon nails pica **Iodine** Endemic goiter hypothyroidism Cretinism in infants severe intellectual disability dwarfism deaf mutism **Zinc** Acrodermatitis enteropathica impaired wound healing blunted taste hypogeusia alopecia immune dysfunction **Management** Direct therapeutic micronutrient replacement oral Ferrous sulfate with Vitamin C Cholecalciferol 60000 IU / week IV / oral Thiamine before glucose Vitamin A capsules 200000 IU Ascorbic acid 500 1000 mg Food fortification iodized salt fortified flours Dietary diversification --- **Q 26 Protein Energy Malnutrition PEM** **Definition Causes** Spectrum of nutritional deficiency disorders resulting from inadequate intake / absorption of protein and caloric energy to meet bodily growth and metabolic needs Caused by poverty food insecurity faulty early weaning onto dilute starches recurrent gastroenteritis and measles / TB infections **Types Clinical Features** **Kwashiorkor Protein deficiency with adequate calories** Generalized bilateral pitting edema moon face crazy pavement / flaky paint dermatosis sparse dry hypopigmented hair with alternating light / dark bands flag sign marked hepatomegaly fatty liver infiltration profound misery apathy and anorexia **Marasmus Severe deficiency of both calories and protein** Severe somatic muscle wasting and total loss of subcutaneous fat wizened shrunken old man facies prominent ribs accordion cage loose folds of redundant skin over buttocks baggy pants absence of edema voracious appetite **Marasmic Kwashiorkor** Severe tissue wasting coexisting with peripheral pitting edema **Complications** Hypoglycemia 54 mg / dL hypothermia 355 C severe electrolyte derangements hypokalemia hypomagnesemia sodium retention occult severe sepsis heart failure and acute keratomalacia **Management WHO 10 Step Guidelines** **Stabilization Days 1 7** Treat hypoglycemia 10 dextrose warm child gentle oral rehydration with ReSoMal no rapid IV fluids supplement potassium / magnesium broad spectrum antibiotics Vitamin A initial starter feeding with low protein F 75 diet 75 kcal / 100 mL strictly withhold iron during stabilization **Rehabilitation Weeks 2 6** Transition to energy dense F 100 diet 100 kcal / 100 mL or RUTF for catch up growth add iron supplementation emotional stimulation --- **PART V MATERNAL HEALTH LABOR GYNECOLOGY Q27 Q32** --- **Q 27 Physiological Changes in Pregnancy Antenatal Postnatal Care** **Physiolo